SELENIUM DROPDOWNS — HANDS-ON

Dropdowns are another high-frequency Selenium interview topic. The most important thing is to first identify whether it is a native HTML <select> dropdown or a custom dropdown.

291. What is the Select class?
Interview answer

“Select is a Selenium class used to handle standard HTML dropdowns implemented using the <select> tag. It provides methods such as selectByVisibleText(), selectByValue(), and selectByIndex().”

Import:

import org.openqa.selenium.support.ui.Select;

Example HTML:

<select id="country">
    <option value="india">India</option>
    <option value="usa">USA</option>
    <option value="uk">UK</option>
</select>

Java:

WebElement country =
        driver.findElement(By.id("country"));

Select select = new Select(country);

Now you can select an option.

🔥 Important: When can you use Select?

Only when the dropdown is a real HTML:

<select>

For example:

<select id="country">

Then:

Select select = new Select(element);

works.

But if the application uses:

<div>
    <button>India</button>
    <ul>
        <li>India</li>
        <li>USA</li>
    </ul>
</div>

then you cannot use Select.

That is a custom dropdown.

292. What is selectByVisibleText()?

It selects an option based on the text displayed to the user.

Example:

<select id="country">
    <option value="IN">India</option>
    <option value="US">United States</option>
    <option value="UK">United Kingdom</option>
</select>

Java:

Select select =
        new Select(driver.findElement(By.id("country")));

select.selectByVisibleText("India");
Why "VisibleText"?

Because you're selecting exactly what the user sees:

India
United States
United Kingdom
⭐ Interview answer

“selectByVisibleText() selects an option based on the visible text displayed in the dropdown.”

293. What is selectByValue()?

It selects an option using the HTML value attribute.

HTML:

<select id="country">
    <option value="IN">India</option>
    <option value="US">United States</option>
    <option value="UK">United Kingdom</option>
</select>

Java:

select.selectByValue("IN");

This selects:

India

because:

value="IN"
⭐ Interview answer

“selectByValue() selects an option using the value attribute of the <option> element.”

Important difference
Visible text:
India

Value:
IN

So:

select.selectByVisibleText("India");

versus:

select.selectByValue("IN");
294. What is selectByIndex()?

It selects an option using its zero-based index.

Example:

<select id="country">
    <option>India</option>       <!-- index 0 -->
    <option>USA</option>         <!-- index 1 -->
    <option>UK</option>          <!-- index 2 -->
</select>

Java:

select.selectByIndex(1);

This selects:

USA
⭐ Important

Index starts from 0.

India → 0
USA   → 1
UK    → 2
Interview answer

“selectByIndex() selects an option using its zero-based index. I use it only when the option order is stable, because it can become fragile if the dropdown order changes.”

🔥 Compare the Three

Suppose:

<option value="IN">India</option>

You can use:

select.selectByVisibleText("India");

or:

select.selectByValue("IN");

or, if India is index 0:

select.selectByIndex(0);
Easy memory:
VisibleText → What user sees

Value       → HTML value attribute

Index       → Position
🔥 295. How do you handle a Custom Dropdown?

This is very important in real projects.

First, don't use:

Select select = new Select(element);

unless it is a <select> element.

For a custom dropdown, you usually:

Click the dropdown.
Locate the available options.
Iterate through them or locate the desired option directly.
Click the required option.
Example Custom Dropdown

HTML might look like:

<div id="countryDropdown">
    <div>India</div>
    <div>USA</div>
    <div>UK</div>
</div>

Or:

<div class="dropdown">India</div>

<ul>
    <li>India</li>
    <li>USA</li>
    <li>UK</li>
</ul>
Selenium approach
driver.findElement(By.id("countryDropdown")).click();

List<WebElement> options =
        driver.findElements(By.xpath("//li"));

for (WebElement option : options) {

    if (option.getText().equals("India")) {
        option.click();
        break;
    }
}
🔥 Better Custom Dropdown Code

Suppose the options are:

<li>India</li>
<li>USA</li>
<li>UK</li>

You can do:

driver.findElement(By.id("countryDropdown")).click();

List<WebElement> options =
        driver.findElements(By.xpath("//li"));

for (WebElement option : options) {

    String text = option.getText().trim();

    if (text.equals("India")) {
        option.click();
        break;
    }
}
⭐ Interview explanation

“For a custom dropdown, I don't use the Selenium Select class. I click the dropdown, locate the option elements, identify the required option using text or a stable attribute, and click it.”

🔥 296. How do you select a Dynamic Dropdown?

Dynamic dropdown means the options are generated or changed dynamically based on user input or application data.

For example:

Select Country
      ↓
India
      ↓
Select State
      ↓
Haryana
Punjab
Maharashtra

The state options may not exist until you select a country.

Example
driver.findElement(By.id("country")).click();

List<WebElement> countries =
        driver.findElements(By.xpath("//li"));

for (WebElement country : countries) {

    if (country.getText().equals("India")) {
        country.click();
        break;
    }
}

Then wait for the dynamically loaded state options:

WebDriverWait wait =
        new WebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(10));

List<WebElement> states = wait.until(
        ExpectedConditions.visibilityOfAllElementsLocatedBy(
                By.xpath("//li")
        )
);

Then select:

for (WebElement state : states) {

    if (state.getText().equals("Haryana")) {
        state.click();
        break;
    }
}
🔥 Real-Time Dynamic Dropdown Scenario

Suppose your application has:

Enter City
     ↓
Ban
     ↓
Bangalore
Banjara Hills
Bandung

The results are loaded dynamically.

You should not immediately try to click the result.

Instead:

driver.findElement(By.id("city"))
      .sendKeys("Ban");

Then wait:

WebDriverWait wait =
        new WebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(10));

List<WebElement> options = wait.until(
    ExpectedConditions.visibilityOfAllElementsLocatedBy(
        By.xpath("//li[contains(@class,'option')]")
    )
);

Then:

for (WebElement option : options) {

    if (option.getText().trim().equals("Bangalore")) {

        option.click();
        break;
    }
}
🔴 297. How do you handle an Auto-Suggest Dropdown?

This is one of the most frequently asked practical Selenium questions.

Auto-suggest:

Enter city:
[Banga________________]

Suggestions:
--------------------
Bangalore
Bangalore Rural
Bangalore Urban
--------------------

You normally:

Step 1

Enter partial text.

driver.findElement(By.id("city"))
      .sendKeys("Banga");
Step 2

Wait for suggestions.

WebDriverWait wait =
        new WebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(10));
Step 3

Get suggestions.

List<WebElement> suggestions =
        wait.until(
            ExpectedConditions.visibilityOfAllElementsLocatedBy(
                By.xpath("//li")
            )
        );
Step 4

Find required suggestion.

for (WebElement suggestion : suggestions) {

    if (suggestion.getText().trim()
            .equalsIgnoreCase("Bangalore")) {

        suggestion.click();
        break;
    }
}
🔥 Complete Auto-Suggest Code
WebElement city =
        driver.findElement(By.id("city"));

city.sendKeys("Banga");

WebDriverWait wait =
        new WebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(10));

List<WebElement> suggestions =
        wait.until(
            ExpectedConditions.visibilityOfAllElementsLocatedBy(
                By.xpath("//li")
            )
        );

for (WebElement suggestion : suggestions) {

    String text = suggestion.getText().trim();

    System.out.println("Suggestion: " + text);

    if (text.equalsIgnoreCase("Bangalore")) {

        suggestion.click();
        break;
    }
}
