🔥 Interview Scenario: "Select the Option Dynamically"

Suppose interviewer says:

"Don't hardcode the index. Select India from the dropdown."

Don't do:

select.selectByIndex(2);

Instead:

for (WebElement option : options) {

    if (option.getText().trim().equals("India")) {
        option.click();
        break;
    }
}

This is more robust when option order changes.

🔥 How to Print All Dropdown Options?

For a standard <select>:

Select select =
        new Select(driver.findElement(By.id("country")));

List<WebElement> options =
        select.getOptions();

for (WebElement option : options) {

    System.out.println(option.getText());
}
🔥 How to Get Selected Option?

Use:

WebElement selected =
        select.getFirstSelectedOption();

System.out.println(selected.getText());

Example:

Select select =
        new Select(driver.findElement(By.id("country")));

select.selectByVisibleText("India");

WebElement selected =
        select.getFirstSelectedOption();

System.out.println("Selected: "
        + selected.getText());
🔥 How to Check Whether Dropdown Supports Multiple Selection?

You can use:

select.isMultiple();

Example:

if (select.isMultiple()) {
    System.out.println("Multiple selection supported");
}

For a multi-select dropdown:

select.selectByVisibleText("India");
select.selectByVisibleText("USA");
🔥 Common Interview Trap
Interviewer:

“Can we use Select class for every dropdown?”

❌ Don't say:

Yes.

✅ Say:

“No. The Select class works only with standard HTML <select> elements. For custom dropdowns implemented using elements such as div, li, buttons, or other components, I handle them using normal Selenium locators and clicks.”

That's an important distinction.

🔥 Standard vs Custom vs Auto-Suggest
Type	Approach
HTML <select>	Select class
Custom dropdown	Click + locate option + click
Dynamic dropdown	Wait for options + locate + click
Auto-suggest	Type text + wait for suggestions + select desired option
🧠 MUST MEMORIZE
Standard dropdown
Select select =
        new Select(driver.findElement(By.id("country")));
By visible text
select.selectByVisibleText("India");
By value
select.selectByValue("IN");
By index
select.selectByIndex(0);
Get all options
List<WebElement> options =
        select.getOptions();
Selected option
select.getFirstSelectedOption();
Multiple selection
select.isMultiple();
